1
00:00:04,597 --> 00:00:08,718
On our arrival in Denmark, we found the king and queen of that country elevated

2
00:00:08,718 --> 00:00:14,800
in two arm-chairs on a kitchen-table, holding a Court. The whole of the Danish nobility were in

3
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:20,917
attendance; consisting of a noble boy in the wash-leather boots of a gigantic ancestor,

4
00:00:20,917 --> 00:00:26,000
a venerable Peer with a dirty face who seemed to have risen from the people late in life,

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,519
and the Danish chivalry with a comb in its hair and a pair of white silk legs,

6
00:00:30,519 --> 00:00:37,120
and presenting on the whole a feminine appearance. My gifted townsman stood gloomily apart,

7
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:43,917
with folded arms, and I could have wished that his curls and forehead had been more probable.

8
00:00:43,917 --> 00:00:49,480
Several curious little circumstances transpired as the action proceeded. The late king of the

9
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:54,398
country not only appeared to have been troubled with a cough at the time of his decease, but to

10
00:00:54,398 --> 00:01:00,358
have taken it with him to the tomb, and to have brought it back. The royal phantom also carried

11
00:01:00,358 --> 00:01:05,718
a ghostly manuscript round its truncheon, to which it had the appearance of occasionally referring,

12
00:01:05,718 --> 00:01:11,120
and that too, with an air of anxiety and a tendency to lose the place of reference which were

13
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:18,038
suggestive of a state of mortality. It was this, I conceive, which led to the Shade’s being advised

14
00:01:18,038 --> 00:01:25,519
by the gallery to “turn over!”—a recommendation which it took extremely ill. It was likewise to

15
00:01:25,519 --> 00:01:30,597
be noted of this majestic spirit, that whereas it always appeared with an air of having been

16
00:01:30,597 --> 00:01:36,398
out a long time and walked an immense distance, it perceptibly came from a closely contiguous

17
00:01:36,398 --> 00:01:43,877
wall. This occasioned its terrors to be received derisively. The Queen of Denmark, a very buxom

18
00:01:43,879 --> 00:01:49,199
lady, though no doubt historically brazen, was considered by the public to have too much brass

19
00:01:49,199 --> 00:01:55,800
about her; her chin being attached to her diadem by a broad band of that metal (as if she had a

20
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:02,319
gorgeous toothache), her waist being encircled by another, and each of her arms by another, so that

21
00:02:02,319 --> 00:02:09,839
she was openly mentioned as “the kettle-drum.” The noble boy in the ancestral boots was inconsistent,

22
00:02:09,839 --> 00:02:15,718
representing himself, as it were in one breath, as an able seaman, a strolling actor,

23
00:02:15,718 --> 00:02:21,961
a grave-digger, a clergyman, and a person of the utmost importance at a Court fencing-match,

24
00:02:21,961 --> 00:02:29,438
on the authority of whose practised eye and nice discrimination the finest strokes were judged.

25
00:02:29,438 --> 00:02:34,758
This gradually led to a want of toleration for him, and even—on his being detected in

26
00:02:34,758 --> 00:02:41,237
holy orders, and declining to perform the funeral service—to the general indignation taking the form

27
00:02:41,237 --> 00:02:49,480
of nuts. Lastly, Ophelia was a prey to such slow musical madness, that when, in course of time,

28
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:55,838
she had taken off her white muslin scarf, folded it up, and buried it, a sulky man who had been

29
00:02:55,839 --> 00:03:01,358
long cooling his impatient nose against an iron bar in the front row of the gallery, growled,

30
00:03:01,358 --> 00:03:08,160
“Now the baby’s put to bed let’s have supper!” Which, to say the least of it, was out of keeping.

31
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:13,917
Upon my unfortunate townsman all these incidents accumulated with playful effect. Whenever that

32
00:03:13,917 --> 00:03:20,038
undecided Prince had to ask a question or state a doubt, the public helped him out with it. As for

33
00:03:20,038 --> 00:03:26,199
example; on the question whether ’twas nobler in the mind to suffer, some roared yes, and some no,

34
00:03:26,199 --> 00:03:32,718
and some inclining to both opinions said “Toss up for it;” and quite a Debating Society arose.

35
00:03:32,718 --> 00:03:36,559
When he asked what should such fellows as he do crawling between earth and heaven,

36
00:03:36,559 --> 00:03:41,319
he was encouraged with loud cries of “Hear, hear!” When he appeared with his stocking

37
00:03:41,319 --> 00:03:47,277
disordered (its disorder expressed, according to usage, by one very neat fold in the top,

38
00:03:47,277 --> 00:03:52,800
which I suppose to be always got up with a flat iron), a conversation took place in the gallery

39
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:57,641
respecting the paleness of his leg, and whether it was occasioned by the turn the ghost had given

40
00:03:57,641 --> 00:04:03,358
him. On his taking the recorders,—very like a little black flute that had just been played

41
00:04:03,358 --> 00:04:09,639
in the orchestra and handed out at the door,—he was called upon unanimously for Rule Britannia.

42
00:04:10,398 --> 00:04:15,557
When he recommended the player not to saw the air thus, the sulky man said, “And don’t you do it,

43
00:04:15,559 --> 00:04:20,800
neither; you’re a deal worse than him!” And I grieve to add that peals of laughter greeted

44
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:28,078
Mr. Wopsle on every one of these occasions. But his greatest trials were in the churchyard,

45
00:04:28,078 --> 00:04:33,277
which had the appearance of a primeval forest, with a kind of small ecclesiastical wash-house

46
00:04:33,277 --> 00:04:40,038
on one side, and a turnpike gate on the other. Mr. Wopsle in a comprehensive black cloak,

47
00:04:40,038 --> 00:04:46,038
being descried entering at the turnpike, the gravedigger was admonished in a friendly way,

48
00:04:46,038 --> 00:04:53,116
“Look out! Here’s the undertaker a coming, to see how you’re a getting on with your work!” I believe

49
00:04:53,116 --> 00:04:58,277
it is well known in a constitutional country that Mr. Wopsle could not possibly have returned the

50
00:04:58,277 --> 00:05:03,879
skull, after moralizing over it, without dusting his fingers on a white napkin taken from his

51
00:05:03,879 --> 00:05:09,559
breast; but even that innocent and indispensable action did not pass without the comment,

52
00:05:10,237 --> 00:05:17,758
“Wai-ter!” The arrival of the body for interment (in an empty black box with the lid tumbling

53
00:05:17,758 --> 00:05:24,680
open), was the signal for a general joy, which was much enhanced by the discovery, among the bearers,

54
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:31,480
of an individual obnoxious to identification. The joy attended Mr. Wopsle through his struggle

55
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:37,000
with Laertes on the brink of the orchestra and the grave, and slackened no more until

56
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,237
he had tumbled the king off the kitchen-table, and had died by inches from the ankles upward.

57
00:05:43,237 --> 00:05:48,597
We had made some pale efforts in the beginning to applaud Mr. Wopsle; but they were too hopeless to

58
00:05:48,597 --> 00:05:55,680
be persisted in. Therefore we had sat, feeling keenly for him, but laughing, nevertheless,

59
00:05:55,680 --> 00:06:02,879
from ear to ear. I laughed in spite of myself all the time, the whole thing was so droll;

60
00:06:02,879 --> 00:06:07,319
and yet I had a latent impression that there was something decidedly fine in Mr. Wopsle’s

61
00:06:07,319 --> 00:06:13,718
elocution,—not for old associations’ sake, I am afraid, but because it was very slow, very dreary,

62
00:06:13,718 --> 00:06:20,116
very uphill and downhill, and very unlike any way in which any man in any natural circumstances of

63
00:06:20,116 --> 00:06:25,480
life or death ever expressed himself about anything. When the tragedy was over, and he

64
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:31,639
had been called for and hooted, I said to Herbert, “Let us go at once, or perhaps we shall meet him.”

65
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:37,480
We made all the haste we could downstairs, but we were not quick enough either. Standing at

66
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:42,000
the door was a Jewish man with an unnatural heavy smear of eyebrow,

67
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,480
who caught my eyes as we advanced, and said, when we came up with him,—

68
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:48,678
“Mr. Pip and friend?”

69
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:51,879
Identity of Mr. Pip and friend confessed.

70
00:06:51,879 --> 00:06:57,237
“Mr. Waldengarver,” said the man, “would be glad to have the honour.”

71
00:06:57,237 --> 00:07:03,038
“Waldengarver?” I repeated—when Herbert murmured in my ear, “Probably Wopsle.”

72
00:07:03,038 --> 00:07:06,641
“Oh!” said I. “Yes. Shall we follow you?”

73
00:07:06,641 --> 00:07:09,641
“A few steps, please.” When we were in a side

74
00:07:09,641 --> 00:07:15,358
alley, he turned and asked, “How did you think he looked?—I dressed him.”

75
00:07:15,358 --> 00:07:21,038
I don’t know what he had looked like, except a funeral; with the addition of a large Danish sun

76
00:07:21,038 --> 00:07:25,879
or star hanging round his neck by a blue ribbon, that had given him the appearance

77
00:07:25,879 --> 00:07:33,160
of being insured in some extraordinary Fire Office. But I said he had looked very nice.

78
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:36,237
“When he come to the grave,” said our conductor,

79
00:07:36,237 --> 00:07:41,038
“he showed his cloak beautiful. But, judging from the wing, it looked to me that when he

80
00:07:41,038 --> 00:07:46,038
see the ghost in the queen’s apartment, he might have made more of his stockings.”

81
00:07:46,038 --> 00:07:50,436
I modestly assented, and we all fell through a little dirty swing door,

82
00:07:50,438 --> 00:07:56,237
into a sort of hot packing-case immediately behind it. Here Mr. Wopsle was divesting

83
00:07:56,237 --> 00:08:01,000
himself of his Danish garments, and here there was just room for us to look at him

84
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:07,116
over one another’s shoulders, by keeping the packing-case door, or lid, wide open.

85
00:08:07,116 --> 00:08:11,961
“Gentlemen,” said Mr. Wopsle, “I am proud to see you. I hope, Mr. Pip,

86
00:08:11,961 --> 00:08:16,358
you will excuse my sending round. I had the happiness to know you in former times,

87
00:08:16,358 --> 00:08:23,038
and the Drama has ever had a claim which has ever been acknowledged, on the noble and the affluent.”

88
00:08:23,038 --> 00:08:26,680
Meanwhile, Mr. Waldengarver, in a frightful perspiration,

89
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:30,277
was trying to get himself out of his princely sables.

90
00:08:30,277 --> 00:08:34,277
“Skin the stockings off Mr. Waldengarver,” said the owner of that property,

91
00:08:34,277 --> 00:08:38,718
“or you’ll bust ’em. Bust ’em, and you’ll bust five-and-thirty shillings. Shakspeare

92
00:08:38,718 --> 00:08:44,480
never was complimented with a finer pair. Keep quiet in your chair now, and leave ’em to me.”

93
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:49,116
With that, he went upon his knees, and began to flay his victim; who,

94
00:08:49,116 --> 00:08:52,639
on the first stocking coming off, would certainly have fallen over

95
00:08:52,641 --> 00:08:57,961
backward with his chair, but for there being no room to fall anyhow.

96
00:08:57,961 --> 00:09:02,641
I had been afraid until then to say a word about the play. But then,

97
00:09:02,641 --> 00:09:06,480
Mr. Waldengarver looked up at us complacently, and said,—

98
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:10,398
“Gentlemen, how did it seem to you, to go, in front?”

99
00:09:10,398 --> 00:09:17,639
Herbert said from behind (at the same time poking me), “Capitally.” So I said “Capitally.”

100
00:09:17,641 --> 00:09:19,358
“How did you like my reading of the character,

101
00:09:19,358 --> 00:09:24,639
gentlemen?” said Mr. Waldengarver, almost, if not quite, with patronage.

102
00:09:24,641 --> 00:09:31,718
Herbert said from behind (again poking me), “Massive and concrete.” So I said boldly,

103
00:09:31,718 --> 00:09:38,839
as if I had originated it, and must beg to insist upon it, “Massive and concrete.”

104
00:09:38,839 --> 00:09:45,718
“I am glad to have your approbation, gentlemen,” said Mr. Waldengarver, with an air of dignity,

105
00:09:45,718 --> 00:09:52,277
in spite of his being ground against the wall at the time, and holding on by the seat of the chair.

106
00:09:52,277 --> 00:09:56,480
“But I’ll tell you one thing, Mr. Waldengarver,” said the man who was on his knees, “in which

107
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:02,917
you’re out in your reading. Now mind! I don’t care who says contrairy; I tell you so. You’re out in

108
00:10:02,917 --> 00:10:08,519
your reading of Hamlet when you get your legs in profile. The last Hamlet as I dressed, made

109
00:10:08,519 --> 00:10:13,237
the same mistakes in his reading at rehearsal, till I got him to put a large red wafer on each

110
00:10:13,237 --> 00:10:19,116
of his shins, and then at that rehearsal (which was the last) I went in front, sir, to the back

111
00:10:19,116 --> 00:10:24,917
of the pit, and whenever his reading brought him into profile, I called out “I don’t see no

112
00:10:24,917 --> 00:10:31,438
wafers!” And at night his reading was lovely.” Mr. Waldengarver smiled at me, as much as to

113
00:10:31,438 --> 00:10:38,680
say “a faithful Dependent—I overlook his folly;” and then said aloud, “My view is a

114
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:44,358
little classic and thoughtful for them here; but they will improve, they will improve.”

115
00:10:44,358 --> 00:10:48,917
Herbert and I said together, O, no doubt they would improve.

116
00:10:48,917 --> 00:10:53,438
“Did you observe, gentlemen,” said Mr. Waldengarver, “that there was a man in

117
00:10:53,438 --> 00:10:59,116
the gallery who endeavoured to cast derision on the service,—I mean, the representation?”

118
00:10:59,116 --> 00:11:01,436
We basely replied that we rather thought we had

119
00:11:01,438 --> 00:11:06,038
noticed such a man. I added, “He was drunk, no doubt.”

120
00:11:06,038 --> 00:11:09,160
“O dear no, sir,” said Mr. Wopsle, “not drunk.

121
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:15,480
His employer would see to that, sir. His employer would not allow him to be drunk.”

122
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:18,038
“You know his employer?” said I.

123
00:11:18,038 --> 00:11:22,480
Mr. Wopsle shut his eyes, and opened them again; performing both ceremonies very

124
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:28,756
slowly. “You must have observed, gentlemen,” said he, “an ignorant and a blatant ass,

125
00:11:28,758 --> 00:11:33,597
with a rasping throat and a countenance expressive of low malignity, who went

126
00:11:33,597 --> 00:11:41,358
through—I will not say sustained—the rôle (if I may use a French expression) of Claudius,

127
00:11:41,358 --> 00:11:48,678
King of Denmark. That is his employer, gentlemen. Such is the profession!”

128
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:53,277
Without distinctly knowing whether I should have been more sorry for Mr. Wopsle if he had been in

129
00:11:53,277 --> 00:11:59,678
despair, I was so sorry for him as it was, that I took the opportunity of his turning

130
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:06,277
round to have his braces put on,—which jostled us out at the doorway,—to ask Herbert what he

131
00:12:06,277 --> 00:12:11,917
thought of having him home to supper? Herbert said he thought it would be kind to do so;

132
00:12:11,917 --> 00:12:16,961
therefore I invited him, and he went to Barnard’s with us, wrapped up to the eyes,

133
00:12:16,961 --> 00:12:21,398
and we did our best for him, and he sat until two o’clock in the morning,

134
00:12:21,398 --> 00:12:27,877
reviewing his success and developing his plans. I forget in detail what they were,

135
00:12:27,879 --> 00:12:33,597
but I have a general recollection that he was to begin with reviving the Drama, and to end with

136
00:12:33,597 --> 00:12:41,277
crushing it; inasmuch as his decease would leave it utterly bereft and without a chance or hope.

137
00:12:41,277 --> 00:12:47,519
Miserably I went to bed after all, and miserably thought of Estella, and miserably dreamed that my

138
00:12:47,519 --> 00:12:53,160
expectations were all cancelled, and that I had to give my hand in marriage to Herbert’s Clara,

139
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:58,839
or play Hamlet to Miss Havisham’s Ghost, before twenty thousand people, without knowing twenty

140
00:12:58,839 --> 00:13:00,000
words of it. Chapter 32.
